"A timing diagram plots participant states or values against an explicit time axis and timing constraints.\n\nUse when: Review deadlines, duration limits, and state overlap. Explain time-sensitive protocol behavior with known or proposed bounds.\n\nAvoid when: Only causal ordering is known and durations would be invented.\n\nVisual grammar: Aligned lanes represent participant state or value histories. Horizontal position encodes time under a stated scale. Intervals and markers encode duration or time constraints with units.\n\nBuild: Choose participants and define the reference clock or relative origin. Populate state changes from a trace or clearly labeled specification. Add deadlines and allowed duration ranges separately from observations. Align shared events vertically and reserve clear space for constraint brackets.\n\nCheck: Every duration has units and an observed or required status. State intervals are continuous where the model requires continuity. Cross-clock comparisons have justified synchronization assumptions. Constraint labels never sit on waveform transitions or lane borders.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing equal spacing that readers mistake for equal elapsed time. Mixing required deadlines and measured timings without distinct labels.\n\nExample: A lease is Valid until its expiry instant; renewal must arrive within a specified interval before that boundary.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, State machine diagram."
